# Child processes

A **child process** is another program that an HQL program starts, writes
to, reads from and waits for. The reading club keeps its notes in a `git`
repository and its shelf in a file, and the tools that already answer
questions about them are programs: `git`, `sort`, `grep`. A script that
starts one reads what it prints as values:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

source(command("printf", ["Dune\nEmma\n"]), Lines)
```

```text
["Dune", "Emma"]
```

`command("printf", ["Dune\nEmma\n"])` names a program and its arguments.
It is an endpoint, as `file(p)` is. `source` starts the program, reads its
standard output to the end, waits for it to exit, and gives each line as a
value. The other operations of `io` accept a command too: `tap` reads the
output as it arrives, and `target` and `sink` write the child's standard
input.

What a child prints is bytes, like the content of a file. A format decodes
it into documents, and a declared record validates them, exactly as for a
file. A child adds one thing a file does not have: it ends with an **exit
status**, and HQL treats every status other than `0` as a failure.

The programs on this page are Unix tools. On Windows, `cmd`, `findstr` or
PowerShell take their place, and the section "Windows" below states what
else differs.

## A command is a value

`command(program, args)` builds a `Command`. Building it starts nothing:

<!-- run: novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

log = command("git", ["log", "--format=%an"])
[log, command("sort")]
```

```text
[command("git", ["log", "--format=%an"]), command("sort")]
```

`io.process` is not part of the prelude. A program that uses `command`
without importing it is rejected before it runs:

<!-- run: novault -->
```hql
command("sort")
```

```text
check error[unknown-name]: `command` is declared in the module `io.process`: write `from io.process import command`
 --> <expression>:1:1
  |
1 | command("sort")
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

Three named arguments describe how the child runs:

| Argument | Meaning | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `dir` | the child's working directory | the directory `hql` runs in |
| `env` | variables added to the child's environment | none |
| `timeout_ms` | the child is terminated after this many milliseconds | `0`, no bound |

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

command("sh", ["-c", "echo $CLUB"], env = {CLUB: "readers"}).run()
| map(o => o.stdout | trim)
```

```text
ok("readers")
```

A `program` without `/` is looked up in `PATH`. A relative `program` with a
`/` resolves against the folder of the source that names it, as every path
does ([Scripts](scripts.hmd)).

Each operation on a command starts the program once. Two `source` calls on
one `Command` are two runs. Bind the result to a name when two steps need
it.

## The five operations

| Operation | The child's standard input | The child's standard output | Gives |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `source(c, F)` | closed | read to its end, decoded by `F` | `Seq<T>` |
| `tap(c, F)` | closed | read as it arrives, decoded by `F` | `Stream<T>` |
| `target(v, c, F)` | `v` as one document, then closed | the program's standard output | `Step` |
| `sink(vs, c, F)` | one document for each value, then closed | the program's standard output | `Step` |
| `c.run(input)` | `input`, then closed | captured | `Result<Output, CommandFailure>` |

`source`, `target`, `sink` and `run` wait for the child to exit. `tap` does
not: it gives each document while the child is still running.

## Reading a child

`source` reads everything the child writes. The format states how the bytes
are documents, and from there the values are refined the way a file's are.
Here a child prints JSON, one document per line, and each document is read
as a `Book`:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

type Book { title: String, pages: Int }

source(command("printf", ["{\"title\": \"Dune\", \"pages\": 412}\n"]), Json)
| map(b => Book(b))
| map(b => b.title)
```

```text
["Dune"]
```

A document that does not fit the contract is an `execute error`, as it is
for a file:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

type Book { title: String, pages: Int }

source(command("printf", ["{\"title\": \"Dune\", \"pages\": \"many\"}\n"]), Json)
| map(b => Book(b))
```

```text
execute error[contract-violation]: this does not read as Book: field `pages`: "many" is not Int
 --> <expression>:6:12
  |
6 | | map(b => Book(b))
  |            ^^^^^^^
  = expected: Book
  = element: {"pages":"many","title":"Dune"}, position 1 of 1 in `map`
  = bindings: b = {"pages":"many","title":"Dune"}
```

`tap` gives a `Stream`, so it also reads a child that never ends
([Finite or live](finite-or-live.hmd)). `yes` prints its argument forever,
and `take(3)` ends the read after three lines:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

tap(command("yes", ["Dune"]), Lines) | take(3)
```

```text
["Dune", "Dune", "Dune"]
```

`yes` is still running when `take` has its three lines. `hql` terminates
it, as the section "Lifetime" below states. A child that must finish its
work is read with `source`.

## Writing to a child

`sink` writes one document to the child's standard input for each value,
and closes it when the values end. The child's own output is not read by
the program. It goes to the program's standard output:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

["Emma", "Dune", "Beloved"] | sink(command("sort"), Lines)
```

```text
Beloved
Dune
Emma
```

The three lines were printed by `sort`. The result of `sink` is a `Step`,
which prints nothing. `target` writes its whole input as one document and
then closes standard input:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

["Emma", "Dune"] | target(command("tr", ["a-z", "A-Z"]), Lines)
```

```text
EMMA
DUNE
```

A command sits in a pipeline like any other endpoint. This one reads a
file, keeps one field and hands the lines to `sort`:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

source(file("examples/io/sightings.jsonl"))
| map(s => s.species)
| sink(command("sort"), Lines)
```

```text
barn owl
barn owl
kingfisher
wren
```

!!! note "A child that reads all its input needs an input that ends"
    `sort` prints nothing until its standard input is closed, and `sink`
    closes it only when its values end. A `sink` to `sort` from a `tap`
    that never ends waits forever. Give such a child a list, a `source`,
    or a stream bounded by `take`.

## `run`

`run` starts the child, writes `input` to its standard input, waits, and
gives the outcome as a `Result`. A child that exits `0` gives `ok` with an
`Output`, the text it wrote on standard output and standard error:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

command("tr", ["a-z", "A-Z"]).run(input = "dune")
```

```text
ok(Output(stdout = "DUNE", stderr = ""))
```

Every other end gives `err` with a `CommandFailure`:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

command("sh", ["-c", "echo no disk >&2; exit 3"]).run()
```

```text
err(CommandFailure(command = command("sh", ["-c", "echo no disk >&2; exit 3"]), reason = "exited", status = 3, signal = none, stdout = "", stderr = "no disk\n"))
```

`Output` has no status, since an `ok` is always a child that exited `0`.
The functions of `Result` work on the outcome
([Handling errors](../12-errors/handling.hmd)). `and_then` starts the
second command only when the first succeeded:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

command("printf", ["dune"]).run()
| and_then(o => command("tr", ["a-z", "A-Z"]).run(input = o.stdout))
| map(o => o.stdout)
```

```text
ok("DUNE")
```

## Failures

A `CommandFailure` states why the command did not succeed in its `reason`:

| `reason` | The failure | Set beside it |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `not-found` | no program of that name, in `PATH` or at the path | |
| `not-permitted` | the file is not executable by this user | |
| `exited` | the child exited with a status other than `0` | `status` |
| `signalled` | a signal ended the child | `signal` |
| `timed-out` | `timeout_ms` passed and `hql` terminated the child | |
| `closed` | under `target` or `sink`, the child closed its standard input before a write ended | |
| `invalid-text` | under `run`, the child wrote bytes that are not UTF-8 | |

`stdout` and `stderr` hold what the child wrote before it ended. A child
that runs past its bound is terminated:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

command("sleep", ["30"], timeout_ms = 200).run() | map_err(e => e.reason)
```

```text
err("timed-out")
```

Some tools use a non-zero status for an ordinary answer. `grep` exits `1`
when no line matches. A program that expects this matches on `status`:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

match command("grep", ["-c", "heron", "examples/io/sightings.jsonl"]).run() {
    ok(o) => o.stdout | trim,
    err(e) given e.status == some(1) => "0",
    err(e) => e.stderr,
}
```

```text
"0"
```

`source`, `tap`, `target` and `sink` give a sequence, a stream and a
`Step`. None of them can hold a status, so a failure under them stops
evaluation:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

source(command("sh", ["-c", "echo no disk >&2; exit 3"]), Lines)
```

```text
no disk
execute failure[io-failure]: command `sh`: failed: exited, status 3
 --> <expression>:3:1
  |
3 | source(command("sh", ["-c", "echo no disk >&2; exit 3"]), Lines)
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  = caused by: its standard error ended with:
               no disk
```

The first line is the child's own standard error, which passes through to
the program's. The diagnostic then names the command, the reason and the
status, and gives the last lines of the child's standard error as the
cause. A program that handles a failure uses `run`.

Either way the shell sees the failure. A program stopped by a failure
exits `1`, and so does a program whose result is the `err` of a `run`
([Scripts](scripts.hmd#the-exit-status)).

## No shell reads the command line

The child receives `program` and `args` exactly as written. No word is
split, no `*` is expanded and no `$NAME` is replaced:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

command("echo", ["*.txt; rm -rf $HOME"]).run() | map(o => o.stdout)
```

```text
ok("*.txt; rm -rf $HOME\n")
```

`echo` received one argument and printed it. Text that comes from outside
the program, a file name or a user's input, is therefore always one
argument and never a command.

The program and its arguments are separate values. `command("ls *.txt")`
names a program called `ls *.txt`, and no such program exists:

<!-- run: unix novault -->
```hql
from io.process import *

command("ls *.txt").run() | map_err(e => e.reason)
```

```text
err("not-found")
```

A pattern of file names is expanded in HQL, with `glob`
([Files and folders](files-and-folders.hmd)), and each match is one
argument. A script that does need shell text names the shell as its
program: `command("sh", ["-c", "ls *.txt"])`.

## The child's environment

A child inherits the environment variables the program itself may read,
and no others. Where the project's `env` grant lists names, the child sees
those names ([Environment and arguments](environment.hmd)). The `env`
argument of `command` adds variables for one child.

## Lifetime

A child does not outlive the evaluation that started it.

```d2
direction: down

value: "command(program, args)\na value, nothing runs" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}
grant: "commands grant\npermits or refuses the program" {style.fill: "#f6efe6"}
start: "source, tap, target, sink or run\nstarts the child" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}
child: "child process\nits own process group" {style.fill: "#f6efe6"}
ok: "exit 0\nthe values, or ok(Output)" {style.fill: "#eef6ee"}
err: "any other end\nevaluation stops, or err(CommandFailure)" {
  style.stroke: "#b23b3b"
  style.stroke-dash: 3
}

value -> grant
grant -> start
start -> child
child -> ok
child -> err
```

`hql` starts each child as the leader of a new process group. Every
process the child starts belongs to that group. `hql` terminates the whole
group when:

- `timeout_ms` passes;
- a read ends before the child does, as under `take`;
- evaluation ends with the child still running, on a failure;
- `hql` itself receives `SIGINT`, `SIGTERM` or `SIGHUP`. A Ctrl-C in the
  terminal is one of these.

Termination is `SIGTERM`, then `SIGKILL` after 5 seconds. A `make` that
`hql` terminates takes the compiler it started with it.

## The `commands` grant

A program that starts other programs has every right of the user who runs
it. A project states which programs its scripts may start, in
`.hql/config.toml`:

```toml
commands = ["git"]
```

Without the key, a run may start every program. With it, a run may start
the programs it lists, matched against `program` as written, and no
other:

```text
$ cat commits.hql
from io.process import *

source(command("sh", ["-c", "git log | wc -l"]), Lines)
$ hql run commits.hql
execute failure[io-failure]: command `sh`: the program is not in the project's `commands` grant
 --> commits.hql:3:1
  |
3 | source(command("sh", ["-c", "git log | wc -l"]), Lines)
  | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
```

A shell is granted like any other program. A list without `sh` or `bash`
keeps shell text out of the project.

`hql render` grants no program. A cell that names a command is rejected
when the document is checked, before any cell runs
([Effects and what a host allows](hosts.hmd)):

```text
check error[type-mismatch]: a document starts no program
 --> report.hmd:5:8
  |
5 | source(command("printf", ["wren"]), Lines)
  |        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
  = cell: 0
```

## Windows

`hql` starts a child on Windows through the Win32 API. It needs no shell
and no POSIX layer.

| | Unix | Windows |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `program` without a path | looked up in `PATH` | the same, and `git` finds `git.exe` |
| Arguments | passed as a list | quoted by `hql` into one command line |
| The child and what it starts | a process group | a Job Object |
| A child that `hql` terminates | `SIGTERM`, then `SIGKILL` after 5 seconds | ended at once, with every process in the job |
| `signal` in a `CommandFailure` | set when a signal ended the child | never set |
| `hql` itself is killed | after `SIGKILL`, the group keeps running | the Job Object ends every process in it |

- On Windows a child that `hql` terminates is ended at once. It receives
  no request to stop first, so it cannot save its work or clean up.
- A `program` ending in `.bat` or `.cmd` is run by `cmd.exe`, which is a
  shell. Under a `commands` grant it starts only when the list names both
  the file and `cmd`: `commands = ["build.bat", "cmd"]`.
- `sort`, `find` and `timeout` are programs of Windows itself, with other
  options than the Unix tools of the same names. `command("sort")` runs
  `sort.exe` from `System32`. Name a program by its path to state which
  one runs.

The [reference](../../reference/std/io/process/index.hmd) states every
type and function of `io.process`.
